From 693161ffc525a9eeea9c8884bc4f3c57a1dca8f2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: iamwrm <iamwrm@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 19 Jul 2026 13:00:10 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 11/16] feat(client): predictive local echo for remote attach
 (mosh-style)

On a 200ms link every keystroke waits a full round trip before echoing.
When the typing context is safe - visible cursor, plain unstyled target
and neighbor cells, room before the right margin - the client now draws
the typed character immediately, underlined like mosh, and reconciles
against later server frames via the screen model (i0002 W3):

- server echoes the same char -> confirmed; the server's own write
  repaints the cell with real styling, dropping the underline
- server writes something else, or a prediction waits >5s -> all
  outstanding predictions are repainted from the model (server truth);
  the model exists exactly because the server diffs against *its* view
  and would never repaint a mispredicted cell it did not touch
- typed-ahead predictions surviving a frame are re-drawn and the host
  cursor is re-anchored past them

Modes via remote.predictive_echo / HERDR_PREDICTIVE_ECHO: off (restores
previous behavior byte-for-byte), always, and auto (default) which
tracks predictions invisibly until two confirmations arrive with >60ms
echo delay - fast links never see underlines, broken contexts never draw.

Only width-1 printable chars (max 8 ahead) and backspace over our own
predictions are predicted; any other key restores server truth first.
Active only for remote full-app terminal-ansi clients. No wire-protocol
change; works against official Linux servers.
